# P3.4 fix — use the ifupdown PACKAGE's /sbin/ifup{,down} (stock parity) instead
# of busybox's applets. Stock ships the real ifupdown package (usr/sbin/ifup is a
# libc-linked ELF); busybox's ifup applet mishandles the /etc/network/interfaces
# pre-up "$IFACE" substitution, so wpa_supplicant is invoked with malformed args
# and dumps its full usage text (twice, once per wlanN stanza) at every boot.
# Disabling the busybox applets lets BR2_PACKAGE_IFUPDOWN's binaries win (with
# BR2_ROOTFS_MERGED_USR, /sbin/ifup == usr/sbin/ifup, exactly stock's path).
# CONFIG_IFUP is not set
# CONFIG_IFDOWN is not set

# util-linux parity (usbmount work, docs/util-linux-parity.md). Stock ships the
# real util-linux ELF versions of all of the tools below (verified against
# work/imgroot), and this build now enables BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_BINARIES + the
# matching program toggles. Two packages that install a program at the same path
# collide, and the last install wins non-deterministically -- so, same idiom as
# ifup/ifdown above, turn OFF each BusyBox applet that util-linux now provides so
# the util-linux binary is the one that lands. Every symbol here has a confirmed
# util-linux replacement (basic set, or an explicit BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_* toggle
# in the defconfig); applets with NO util-linux equivalent are deliberately left ON.
# GETTY is off because the serial console moves to util-linux agetty (inittab).
# CONFIG_BLKID is not set
# CONFIG_DMESG is not set
# CONFIG_FDISK is not set
# CONFIG_FLOCK is not set
# CONFIG_FSFREEZE is not set
# CONFIG_FSTRIM is not set
# CONFIG_GETOPT is not set
# CONFIG_GETTY is not set
# CONFIG_HEXDUMP is not set
# CONFIG_HWCLOCK is not set
# CONFIG_KILL is not set
# CONFIG_MKSWAP is not set
# CONFIG_MORE is not set
# CONFIG_MOUNT is not set
# CONFIG_MOUNTPOINT is not set
# CONFIG_NOLOGIN is not set
# CONFIG_READPROFILE is not set
# CONFIG_RENICE is not set
# CONFIG_SETARCH is not set
# CONFIG_SETSID is not set
# CONFIG_SWAPON is not set
# CONFIG_SWAPOFF is not set
# CONFIG_SWITCH_ROOT is not set
# CONFIG_UMOUNT is not set
# CONFIG_CHRT is not set

# T5 -- `nc` (and its `netcat` alias) is a DELIBERATE SUBSTITUTION, not a
# reproduction, and it is the only part of T5 that still stands. Do not let a
# "stock ships these nine tools" shorthand elsewhere in this repo mislead:
# the other eight names this block used to add -- stat, timeout, tac, shuf,
# comm, split, expand, groups -- are NOT BusyBox applets here any more. GNU
# coreutils now owns those paths on this image exactly as it does on stock,
# and every coreutils applet, those eight included, is disabled in the "GNU
# coreutils owns these paths" block below (see also
# docs/package-manifest.md §4f). `timeout N cmd` and `stat -c '%s' file` are
# answered by the real GNU binaries, so no feature-symbol tuning is needed or
# wanted here.
#
# stock's provider for `nc` is a SEPARATE GNU TOOL, not BusyBox. Do not let the
# "stock ships all nine" shorthand elsewhere in this repo mislead either.
# work/imgroot/usr/bin/netcat is a 34376-byte ARM ELF whose strings include
# "GNU netcat %s, a rewrite of the famous networking tool." and "netcat (The
# GNU Netcat) %s" -- i.e. GNU Netcat, exactly what Buildroot's own
# package/netcat provides (netcat.mk:7, NETCAT_VERSION = 0.7.1) -- and
# usr/bin/nc is a symlink -> netcat beside it. BusyBox's nc is a different
# implementation with a different option set (see the NC_* sub-feature notes
# below), so this trades byte-for-byte parity for ~11 kB instead of a whole
# extra package. That is the intended call: `nc` on a MiSTer is for ad-hoc
# on-device network poking, not for anything that depends on GNU Netcat's
# exact flags. If a real GNU-Netcat dependency ever turns up, the fix is
# BR2_PACKAGE_NETCAT in the defconfig plus turning CONFIG_NC/CONFIG_NETCAT
# back off here -- not patching around it.
# Stock ships BOTH names (`nc` and `netcat`), and CONFIG_NC alone would only
# give the first: BusyBox has a separate alias applet for the second
# (networking/nc.c:17-21 "config NETCAT / bool 'netcat (11 kb)' / default n /
# Alias to nc.", declared at nc.c:51 as
# APPLET_ODDNAME(netcat, nc, BB_DIR_USR_BIN, ...) -> the same usr/bin/netcat
# path stock uses). It costs one more applet-table entry and one more symlink
# -- the 11 kB of code is shared with CONFIG_NC, not paid twice -- so both are
# turned on below and neither of stock's two names 404s.
# No collision with a real package: nothing in this defconfig sets
# BR2_PACKAGE_NETCAT or BR2_PACKAGE_NETCAT_OPENBSD (both exist in this
# Buildroot tree, package/netcat{,-openbsd}/, and would collide with `nc` at
# the same usr/bin/nc path exactly like the LSOF/LSUSB/MKDOSFS/{CHVT,
# DEALLOCVT,OPENVT,SETKEYCODES} block below) -- confirmed by grepping this
# file's own defconfig for both symbols, neither is present.
#
# `nc` gates real functionality behind its OWN sub-feature symbols, and the
# base config (package/busybox/busybox.config) pins each of them EXPLICITLY
# off -- not merely silent-by-default. An explicit "is not set" is a recorded
# choice that Kconfig's oldconfig/olddefconfig NEVER overrides with a symbol's
# own "default y" (it only fills in symbols that have no prior state at all),
# so turning NC on by itself, without also turning these on, would silently
# ship a crippled netcat:
#   - nc: NC_SERVER gates `-l` (listen/server mode), NC_EXTRA gates
#     `-e`/`-i`/`-w`, and NC_110_COMPAT gates `-s`/`-n`/`-u`/`-v`/`-z` --
#     i.e. UDP mode and verbose output. All THREE were off in the base
#     config; a netcat that can neither listen nor speak UDP is not much of
#     a netcat for on-device debugging, so all three are turned on below.
CONFIG_NC=y
CONFIG_NETCAT=y
CONFIG_NC_SERVER=y
CONFIG_NC_EXTRA=y
CONFIG_NC_110_COMPAT=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GNU coreutils owns these paths (stock binary parity Tier 1 -- issue #279
# follow-up, 2026-10-31). Stock ships GNU coreutils 8.32: in the extracted
# 20260907 stock image (docs/stock-inventory/20260907), usr/bin/{stat,timeout,
# tac,shuf,comm,split,expand,groups,...} are symlinks to the usr/bin/coreutils
# multicall binary, and so are the other 76 names below.
# package/mister-userspace/Config.in now selects BR2_PACKAGE_COREUTILS, which
# installs that multicall binary plus one symlink per applet at the same
# usr/bin paths BusyBox's applets claim. Two owners of one path is the
# non-deterministic "last install wins" race this fragment exists to prevent,
# so every BusyBox applet with a GNU coreutils counterpart is turned off here;
# same idiom as the ifupdown, util-linux, wget and tar blocks.
# 84 symbols for 85 applet names: CONFIG_TEST covers BOTH `test` and its `[`
# alias applet (BusyBox declares `[` as an alias of test), so it is listed
# once. pr, join, stdbuf, users and vdir have no BusyBox applet at all, so
# there is nothing to disable -- they simply arrive with the package.
# `kill` is deliberately absent from this list: GNU coreutils builds one too,
# so ownership is not single-valued, and coreutils exposes no kill toggle to
# turn off. Stock's provider is util-linux, which is why the util-linux block
# above disables CONFIG_KILL. Also handled elsewhere: ping/ping6/arping
# (iputils, below).
